<clickhouse>
    <!--
        TESTING topology: a SINGLE ClickHouse node, so it may take a far larger
        share than any one node of the six-node cluster.

        Selected by CH_MEMORY_CONFIG=memory.local.xml (the default). The six-node
        cap lives in memory.xml, and the one-node-per-machine cap for intended
        mode lives in memory.distributed.xml (selected by .env.intended).

        2.5 GB, leaving room for MongoDB, Keeper, PostgreSQL and all five pipeline
        layers on the same machine.

        ── History: raised to 3.5 GB, then put back ─────────────────────────────
        This was briefly 3.5 GB. The reason no longer exists, and the raise is now
        actively harmful, so it has been reverted.

        The raise was made when gold moved from Oracle to PostgreSQL, on two
        grounds. First, Oracle's ~1.1 GB was freed (PostgreSQL holds ~26 MB in the
        same role). Second, and the real driver: the widest per-user query —
        radar_agrifood's 100-keyword filter, a multiSearchAny plus an arrayExists
        over the stemmed article text — peaked at ~2.5 GB on the 30-day seed and
        failed reproducibly with

            Code: 241. DB::Exception: Memory limit (total) exceeded

        THAT QUERY NO LONGER EXISTS. Unifying silver -> gold on Spark moved the geo
        and keyword predicates out of SQL entirely: ClickHouse is now only asked
        for partitioned column reads over JDBC, and Spark evaluates the filters.
        Neither multiSearchAny nor arrayExists appears anywhere in the Python
        sources any more. Measured 2026-08-16 over three hours of live operation:
        735 queries, peak query memory 74.54 MiB — roughly 1/34th of the figure
        the raise was sized for.

        The freed Oracle GB is also no longer free. Spark's driver JVM now runs
        RESIDENT inside the processing container in local[*] mode, in the same
        Docker VM, and it is the largest single consumer here. Leaving 3.5 GB
        reserved for a workload ClickHouse no longer runs squeezes the JVM instead
        — and when the host runs short, the kernel kills the biggest process,
        which is that JVM. That failure was observed on 2026-08-16: the JVM died
        at 19:44:32 and the gold layer stopped advancing. (spark_gold._spark() now
        rebuilds a dead session, so it recovers either way — but not provoking it
        is better than recovering from it.)
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>2500000000</max_server_memory_usage>
    <mark_cache_size>268435456</mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
</clickhouse>
